+## B1 / E7 redo: the displacement bitmap (L)
+
+Status: done. **Verdict: not adopted** (rule 4 fails in both versions: InsertSteady Ir/op +2.7% / +1.2%; every other part of the rule holds).
+
+Design: one bit per table line ("this line may hold a displaced entry"), 32 KB per 1M slots (1M slots / 4 per line = 262,144 bits), plus a fixed array of per-stripe clear counters (begun, done). A writer sets the bit of the line it places a displaced entry in after the entry's word0 is in place (hidden, word1 busy) and before word1 is published (in `install`, the one choke point: new keys, relocation copies, stash placements). A sweep that finds a line with its bit set and no displaced word0 clears it as: begun++, clear, rescan, set again if a displaced word0 is there, done++. A lookup that misses its primary line reads done, the bit, then begun; it skips the alternate line only if the bit is clear and begun == done (no clear overlapped the bit's read). ConstantTime paths read both lines as before.
+
+Measurement code (not part of the change): a lab benchmark `BenchmarkLabContainsAbsentBig`, ContainsAbsent's loop on a Capacity-4M table (~75 MiB, past the 32 MiB LL), in both arms, because the stock ContainsAbsent table (18.8 MiB) fits the LL and reads 0 LL misses/op.
+
+Decision rule (candidate only if all hold):
+1. Answers identical: the gates pass with no diff, except that `tfbench -exact`'s `memory` line may show bytes higher by exactly the bitmap and counters (Bytes() must count them); no other line may differ.
+2. Skip rate (deterministic test): at 85% load at least 30% of 1M absent keys find their alternate line's bit clear (50%, 90%, 94% reported).
+3. LL misses/op of LabContainsAbsentBig at least 20% lower than HEAD (cachegrind, `--LL=33554432,16,64`, n = 50000/150000).
+4. Ir/op of InsertSteady, CheckAndTouchMissInsert, InsertNearFull and InsertSaturated each at most 1% higher than HEAD (ContainsAbsent and CheckAndTouchHit reported).
+5. `verify/tla.sh DispBitmap`: the committed model passes at the sizes tla.sh uses; each guard undone (no set-before-visible; no sequence check) violates the invariant.
+6. The new racy test passes `go test -race -count=20`; `verify/replay.sh stress 300` passes (a TestAbandonedMoveKeepsCounts failure of the documented shape, one round one count high, is not the change's).
+Reported, not judged: ab.sh 15 rounds on ContainsAbsent and InsertSteady.
+
+B1, first version (patch as first measured): InsertSteady Ir/op 1865.0 → 1914.8 (+2.7%; per function: locateAt +32.3, install +11.0, sweepDisp +1.1), so **rule 4 fails for this version** (full results below). A second version, **B1b**, is measured under the **same rule (1–6, unchanged)**, written here before it is measured: mayDisp reads the bit first and returns at once if it is set; only a clear bit takes done, the bit again and begun (the TLA+ reader updated to match); install's setDisp takes the line by an unsigned shift. B1b's patch replaces B1's at `speed-b1-dispbitmap.patch`; B1's first version is kept as `speed-b1-dispbitmap-v1.patch`.
+
+### B1 results
+
+Commands: gates (`/tmp/lab/gates.sh`); `TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestDispBitmapSkipRate$' -v .`; `/tmp/lab/cg.sh <arm> <Bench> 50000 150000` (arms built with `speed-b1-labbench.patch` added); `go test -race -count=20 -run '^(TestDispBitmapSweepRace|TestDispClearRacingPlacement|TestDispBitsCoverDisplaced)$' .`; `CGO_ENABLED=1 verify/replay.sh stress 300`; `verify/tla.sh DispBitmap`; `verify/ab.sh HEAD HEAD+verify/experiments/speed-b1-dispbitmap.patch '^Benchmark(ContainsAbsent|InsertSteady)$' 15`.
+
+1. **Answers: identical.** sanity passes (65 s; it first failed on gofmt and on `TestMemoryOnFirstUse`, which asserted Bytes() = the table alone, so the patch updates that test to the table + bitmap + counters). `tfbench -exact`: one line differs, `memory ... bytes=3764736` → `3773120` (+8,384 B = bitmap 7,360 B for 58,824 lines + counters 1,024 B), as the rule allows. The answer digest differs in the `end=` field of all 30 cells and nowhere else; `end` hashes `f.Bytes()` with the counters (answers_test.go:179). With Bytes() not counting the bitmap the digest has **no diff** (all 30 cells), so every answer is identical. Strictly the rule allowed only the exact gate's memory line, and the digest's `end` carries Bytes() too: an oversight in the rule's wording, reported here, not used to pass anything (the verdict rests on rule 4).
+2. **Skip rate** (1M absent keys, no sweeps; share of lines with the bit set in brackets): 50% load 92.3% (7.7%); **85% load 51.3%** (48.7%); 90% load 41.7% (58.2%); 94% load 33.0% (67.0%). Holds (≥ 30%).
+3. **LL misses/op, LabContainsAbsentBig** (75 MiB table): HEAD 1.154, B1 0.827, B1b 0.827: **−28.3%**. Holds (≥ 20%). D1 misses/op rise (2.088 → 3.344 B1, 2.398 B1b): the bitmap read misses L1 but stays in the LL.
+4. **Ir/op** (cachegrind; HEAD / B1 / B1b; in brackets the library's functions only, without runtime background):
+
+| Benchmark | HEAD | B1 | B1b | B1b vs HEAD |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| InsertSteady | 1865.0 (1756.6) | 1914.8 (1799.0) | 1886.6 (1812.6) | **+1.16%** (lib +3.2%) |
+| CheckAndTouchMissInsert | 9433.5 (9310.4) | 9453.3 (9331.0) | 9464.1 (9344.3) | +0.32% |
+| InsertNearFull | 29442.5 (29296.3) | 29498.1 (29356.2) | 29511.2 (29370.2) | +0.23% |
+| InsertSaturated | 10547.4 (8478.4) | 10592.1 (8487.7) | 10587.7 (8501.1) | +0.38% |
+| ContainsAbsent | 359.1 (358.0) | 345.3 (346.2) | 353.1 (352.7) | −1.7% |
+| CheckAndTouchHit | 518.2 (519.3) | 519.5 (521.7) | 520.4 (522.3) | +0.4% |
+| LabContainsAbsentBig | 358.4 (358.0) | 359.8 (346.4) | 352.4 (352.8) | −1.7% |
+
+   **Fails for InsertSteady** in both versions (+2.7%, +1.16%; bar 1%). Per function (InsertSteady): locateAt +32 (B1) / +46 (B1b), install +11: InsertSteady's new keys miss their primary line two or three times per insert (lock-free lookup, lookupStable, claim), and in steady state about half the lines carry the bit, so the check is paid more often than the alternate line is skipped. B1b's fast path for a set bit made the library's share worse (+3.2% against +2.4%); its smaller total is runtime background (sysmon, nanotime: −13 Ir/op), which is why the table also gives library-only counts.
+5. **TLA+** `verify/tla.sh DispBitmap` (B1b's reader): the protocol passes at 4 sizes (566,029 / 10,156,426 / 2,668,777 / 14,881,571 distinct states: writers {K} or {K, X}, 1 or 2 sweepers); NeverCleared, NeverSkipped, NeverRaced each reached; noSetBefore, noSeq, noRescan each violate NoMiss. 8 m 23 s (B1's reader: the same outcomes, 5 m 35 s). Holds.
+6. **Concurrency**: the racy test (`TestDispBitmapSweepRace`: 256 keys read by 4 readers while fills to 80%, 20 churn batches swept at a clock past their horizon, and a fill to 98% relocate them; ~27,800 bit clears a run; with the bitmap disabled it passes too, so its misses would be the bitmap's; its deterministic companion `TestDispClearRacingPlacement` fails when the sequence check is removed) passes `-race -count=20` (204 s). `replay.sh stress 300`: 0/300 for each of the four racy tests (5 m 43 s). Holds.
+
+Reported (ab.sh, 15 rounds, B1b, ns/op medians): InsertSteady 357.0 → 366.7 (1.03), ContainsAbsent 87.6 → 82.9 (0.95): inside this host's noise.
+
+What was learned: the bitmap does what it should for absent lookups on tables past the LL (28% fewer LL misses/op) and the four review points are met (sequence check, model with failing guards, Bytes() counts 32 KB per 1M slots, the white-box test sets the bit), but the check costs inserts more than the 1% bar: a lookup's primary miss is an insert's normal case. A version that checks the bit only in ContainsAt/CountAt (reads), not on insert paths, would avoid that cost; not tried here.
+
+Patches: `go/verify/experiments/speed-b1-dispbitmap.patch` (B1b), `speed-b1-dispbitmap-v1.patch` (B1, first version), `speed-b1-labbench.patch` (the lab benchmark).
