+## S6: scalar SipHash-2-4 (S)
+
+Status: done. **Verdict: not adopted** (rule 2 fails on the 33-byte string in both versions: −1.9%, −6.3%; HashUint64 −13.8% and the 11- and 21-byte strings −14.5% to −20.6% hold).
+
+Change (internal/siphash): the finalization written into each hash (no `sipFinish` call), the 8-byte path specialized, message words and the tail read without per-byte loops or repeated bounds checks (a string of 8 bytes or more takes its tail from one overlapping 8-byte load), rotations via math/bits as now.
+
+Decision rule:
+1. Outputs identical: the package's vectors pass, and a test compares the new HashUint64 and HashString with the old code (kept in the test) on 1M random inputs each (strings of every length 0–64, random keys).
+2. Ir/op at least 10% lower than HEAD for each SipHash case: BenchmarkHashUint64/siphash and BenchmarkHashString/siphash/{11B,21B,33B} (cachegrind, n = 500000/1500000, the hashes being short).
+3. The gates pass with no diff (Config.Key filters hash through it).
+
+S6, first version (`speed-s6-siphash-v1.patch`; index loop `for i+8 <= n` with an unsafe 8-byte load, finalization written out, overlapping tail load): outputs identical (vectors; `TestSipHashMatchesOld` 1M random keys/inputs, strings 0–64 bytes). Ir/op HEAD → S6: HashUint64/siphash 210.0 → 181.1 (−13.8%), HashString/siphash/11B 261.0 → 223.1 (−14.5%), 21B 326.0 → 269.1 (−17.5%), **33B 368.1 → 361.1 (−1.9%): rule 2 fails** for this version. Per function, the finalization's call is gone (sipFinish 115 → 0) but each 8-byte block costs ~46 Ir against ~38 in the old slice loop. A second version, **S6b**, is measured under the **same rule (1–3, unchanged)**, written here before it is measured: the old slice loop (`for len(b) >= 8 { ...; b = b[8:] }`) with the finalization still written out and the overlapping tail load.
+
+### S6b results
+
+Commands: `go test ./internal/siphash/`; `/tmp/lab/cg2.sh {head6,s6b} <case> '<bench regex>' 500000 1500000`; `/tmp/lab/gates.sh s6b`.
+
+1. Outputs identical: the vectors and `TestSipHashMatchesOld` (1M random keys, uint64 inputs and strings of every length 0–64, against the old code kept in the test) pass. Holds.
+2. Ir/op HEAD → S6b: HashUint64/siphash 210.0 → 181.0 (**−13.8%**); HashString/siphash/11B 261.0 → 216.1 (**−17.2%**); 21B 326.0 → 259.0 (**−20.6%**); **33B 368.1 → 345.0 (−6.3%): fails.**
+3. Gates pass with no diff (sanity 52 s, digest 65 s, exact 24 s). Holds.
+
+What was learned: the finalization's call and arguments cost ~25–40 Ir per hash, a sixth of a short hash; writing it out removes that, and the overlapping tail load replaces the byte loop. On a 33-byte key (4 blocks, 1 tail byte) the per-block cost dominates and the body with everything inlined keeps more state live: per function, 33B HashString 222 + sipFinish 115 = 337 Ir on HEAD against 314 in S6b, while the 4-round blocks cost a little more each than in the old loop. The gain is real for keys under ~24 bytes (IPs, short ids) and small past that.
+
+Patches: `go/verify/experiments/speed-s6-siphash.patch` (S6b), `speed-s6-siphash-v1.patch` (S6, first version).
