+### P1. S5: bounds checks and inlining on the hot paths
+
+Verdict: **candidate** (pre-registered rule). Patch `speed-s5-bce-inline.patch`, go/slots.go +4/−3 and go/touch.go +13/−2; no new tests.
+
+What it changes. Three edits on the paths every touch and count takes. They came from `-d=ssa/check_bce/debug=1` and `-m=2` listings and a per-function cachegrind profile of CheckAndTouchHit on HEAD: touch 238 Ir, bump 107, locateAt 71, nextRegU 51, the loop 30, slotWords 11, farAhead 11.
+1. `touch` calls `slotWords` only when warm handed it a slot, and `farAhead` only when the touch is past its register's unit. Those are the only cases where either can return anything but its early answer: slotWords(-1) is "no match", and farAhead with nu ≤ lu is (0, false, false). CheckAndTouchAt passes no warm slot, and most touches land in their register's unit, so both calls were pure overhead on the hit path. (An inlinable farAhead wrapper doesn't fit Go's inlining budget: its call to the out-of-line part alone costs 57 of the 80.)
+2. `slotWords` reads its two words through one 2-element slice, so one slice check replaces three index checks. In the assembly (`-gcflags=-S`), slotWords' bounds-panic sites go from 6 to 4.
+3. `estimateAt` answers a register (as opposed to a hot entry) with a single `estimate` call. For a register, estimate never fails and never reads the key identity (viewOf needs it only for a hot entry), so the 8-try loop and `stashID` (a multiply and shifts) were dead work there.
+
+```go
+// touch.go, in touch
+if first && !ct && i0 >= 0 { // (slotWords of no slot is no match: skip the call)
+...
+var old uint64
+var far, wait bool
+if nu > lu { // else farAhead's answer is (0, false, false): skip the call
+	old, far, wait = f.farAhead(i, nu, lu, stale)
+}
+// touch.go, in estimateAt
+if !isHot(w1) {
+	r, _ := f.estimate(0, w1, last, now, window, sat)
+	return r
+}
+// slots.go, in slotWords
+w := f.words[i : i+2 : i+2] // one bounds check for the three loads
+```
+
+Why answers can't move: each skipped call is one whose result is a constant in exactly the skipped case. The slice reads the same two words with the same atomic loads in the same order.
+
+Rule: gates no diff; Ir/op of the six benchmarks below each at most +0.5%, and at least two of them −3% or more. Numbers (library-only, total in brackets):
+
+| Benchmark | HEAD | S5 | change |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| CheckAndTouchHit | 519.3 (520.3) | 476.3 (476.5) | **−8.3%** (−8.4%) |
+| CheckAndTouchHitCached | 563.6 (563.8) | 522.6 (522.6) | **−7.3%** (−7.3%) |
+| TouchAndCount | 691.3 (692.1) | 610.3 (610.4) | **−11.7%** (−11.8%) |
+| VelocityCount | 464.8 (465.2) | 420.8 (421.3) | **−9.5%** (−9.4%) |
+| InsertSteady | 1756.3 (1819.3) | 1724.3 (1782.3) | −1.8% (−2.0%) |
+| ContainsAbsent | 358.0 (360.4) | 358.0 (357.9) | −0.01% (−0.7%) |
+
+Gates: sanity 65 s, digest 70 s, exact 31 s, no diff. Timing on this host (ab.sh, 15 rounds, medians, reported only): TouchAndCount 161.1 → 152.6 ns/op (0.95), CheckAndTouchHit 130.3 → 128.0 (0.98), VelocityCount 107.3 → 105.1 (0.98). That is inside this host's noise, in the right direction.
