+### P3. S7: multi-lane SipHash-2-4 for batches (AVX-512)
+
+Verdict: **candidate**, by the rule's timing branch, which was written before measuring for the case where cachegrind can't run AVX-512. Patch `speed-s7-siphash-avx512.patch`, +229/−0, all new code:
+- go/internal/siphash/batch.go (`Key.HashUint64s`)
+- batch_amd64.go and batch_amd64.s (`hash8x`, `cpuHasAVX512F`)
+- batch_other.go (no AVX-512 off amd64)
+- go/filter.go +14 (`Filter.HashUint64s`)
+- tests: go/internal/siphash/batch_test.go and go/hashbatch_test.go
+
+What it is. A new API, `func (f *Filter) HashUint64s(keys, out []uint64)`; each `out[i]` equals `f.HashUint64(keys[i])`.
+- With Config.Key on a CPU with AVX-512F, `hash8x` hashes 8 keys per zmm register: SipHash-2-4's 14-instruction round on 8 lanes, with VPROLQ for the 64-bit rotates.
+- The keyed initial state, the length block 8<<56 and 0xff are broadcast once per call. The final XOR of four values is one VPXORQ and one VPTERNLOGQ $0x96.
+- The remainder, keyed filters on other CPUs, and every non-amd64 build use the scalar `HashUint64`. Unkeyed filters use the maphash loop.
+- Detection runs once (`cpuHasAVX512F`): CPUID max leaf ≥ 7, OSXSAVE, XGETBV's XCR0 & 0xE6 = 0xE6 (SSE, AVX, opmask, the upper zmm halves and zmm16–31), and CPUID.7.0:EBX bit 16.
+
+```asm
+#define ROUND \
+	VPADDQ Z1, Z0, Z0; VPROLQ $13, Z1, Z1; VPXORQ Z0, Z1, Z1; VPROLQ $32, Z0, Z0; \
+	VPADDQ Z3, Z2, Z2; VPROLQ $16, Z3, Z3; VPXORQ Z2, Z3, Z3; \
+	VPADDQ Z3, Z0, Z0; VPROLQ $21, Z3, Z3; VPXORQ Z0, Z3, Z3; \
+	VPADDQ Z1, Z2, Z2; VPROLQ $17, Z1, Z1; VPXORQ Z2, Z1, Z1; VPROLQ $32, Z2, Z2
+```
+
+Evidence: `TestHashUint64sMatches` covers every batch length 0–130, then 1M keys in batches of random lengths under random SipHash keys. It runs once through hash8x and once with AVX-512 forced off, and checks that entries past the batch are left untouched. `TestHashUint64sMatchesFilter` covers lengths 0–130 with and without Config.Key. `go vet` (asmdecl) passes, the package builds for arm64 and vets for 386, and the gates show no diff (sanity 54 s, digest 66 s, exact 25 s). Valgrind's CPUID reports no AVX-512, so cachegrind runs only the scalar path: the test under `valgrind --tool=none` logs `AVX-512F: false`.
+
