+### P6. S3: the overflow table's add, word by word
+
+Verdict: **candidate on Ir alone** (pre-registered rule; its flood part failed, so no flood gain is claimed). Patch `speed-s3-ghost-add-words.patch`, go/ghost.go +29/−14; no new tests.
+
+What it is. `ghost.add` used to load one bucket word per entry and compute `k/3` and `k%3` for each of the 23 entries. It now loads each word once and decodes its three entries from that one load, in the same entry order. A word whose entries are all empty is skipped: it counts as the bucket's first free entry if none was found yet, and word 0's floor lane is left out. A merge's CAS still compares the whole word as read.
+
+```go
+for wi := range ghWords {
+	w := &g.words[base+wi]
+	old := atomic.LoadUint64(w)
+	k, sh := wi*ghPerWord, uint(0)
+	if wi == 0 {
+		k, sh = 1, ghEntryBits
+	}
+	if old>>sh == 0 {
+		if free[bi] < 0 {
+			free[bi] = k
+		}
+		continue
+	}
+	for ; sh < ghPerWord*ghEntryBits; k, sh = k+1, sh+ghEntryBits {
+		e := old >> sh & ghEntryMask
+		// ... the same body as before (free, live, merge, victim)
+	}
+}
+```
+
+Why answers can't move: the entry order, the free and victim choices and the CAS operand are all the same. Three loads of one word are replaced by one load whose value all three decodes use. That is an interleaving the old code also allowed, since Go's atomics are sequentially consistent.
+
+Numbers:
+- `BenchmarkLabGhostAdd` (`speed-s3-labbench.patch`: a 1,024-bucket overflow table filled first, then one add per op of one of 65,536 histories, counts 1–4): 3275.4 → 2917.4 Ir/op (**−10.9%**; library-only the same). D1 misses/op 0.458 → 0.457.
+- heavy16x pairs: 1.022, 1.033, 0.932, 0.922, 0.929, 1.149 (1 of 6 ≥ 1.05; reported).
+- Gates: no diff (sanity 52 s, digest 68 s, exact 24 s).
+
+Limits: the add is a small share of a flood's time next to the scan (S2/S2d), so expect little in whole-flood numbers.
+Quiet-host reading: LabGhostAdd, and heavy16x together with S2d.
+
+## Summary
+
+Every patch under `go/verify/experiments/speed-*.patch` applies to main (63b118d) with `git apply --directory=go`. The passing experiments are written up in full in **Passing experiments** above. Ir/op is cachegrind's, n = 50000/150000 (SipHash 500000/1500000); floods are at a quarter of the paper's scale (n = 65,536).
